<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2015/Barn-Cat.jpg" alt="Painting of a barn cat" width="350" height="465" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Barn Cat. 12x9, oil on linen panel. 2015.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">Composition and palette are fundamental elements of my work. </span>Today I revisited an <a href="http://www.portraitsbysimonbland.com/blog/post/Barn-Cat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">older painting</a>&nbsp;<span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">that I thought would benefit from a different approach to both.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">The original panel was divided into a light half and a dark half. The barn door in shadow became too dominant and no amount of repainting, of which there was a lot, could overcome the basic flaws in the design.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">In this composition I removed most of the barn door (it was also difficult to paint) and included some foreground to break the symmetry. It has one of the most powerful underlying designs of any of my works.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">Working on a textured panel was mostly helpful. I was able to convey a sense of light on the gravel using strokes of thick paint over a thin, warm under painting. The shadow areas were painted with thin paint, so the texture of the panel worked against me a bit.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">Light/dark edges made the</span><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">&nbsp;dappled light in the foreground the most difficult part to paint. After letting the painting sit, I cropped the panel down to a smaller size to eliminate some distracting passages.</span></p>